# Reference output for the headless renderer.
#
# `--headless` renders on a fixed timestep with no terminal control, so a fixed
# seed produces the same bytes every time. Checking those bytes against a
# committed hash is what turns that property into a test with an opinion: the
# run-to-run comparison beside it only says a flight is repeatable, and a
# change that quietly alters every frame is repeatable too.
#
# Checked two ways, from one file. `cargo test --test golden` reproduces the
# bytes in process, so a renderer that has drifted fails on the machine that
# drifted it; the `headless` CI job produces the same files from a release
# binary and runs `sha256sum -c` over this, which also proves the two agree.
#
# Ten flights, sharing
#
#     --headless --frames 120 --seed 1 --size 120x36
#
# and differing in what they ask the renderer to draw:
#
#     truecolor.txt   --demo --color truecolor
#     ascii.txt       --demo --color ascii
#     ansi256.txt     --demo --color 256
#     warp.txt        --engage --throttle 1.0 --color truecolor
#     side.txt        --engage --throttle 1.0 --view side --color truecolor
#     orbit.txt       the same, --orbit 55,35,20
#     astern.txt      the same, --orbit -75,6,20
#     steer.txt       --demo --fps 10 --color truecolor
#     drift.txt       the same, --view side --orbit -60,0,0
#     ahead.txt       --engage --throttle 1.0 --fps 10 --view side --orbit 90,0,0
#
# All ten moved when the sky stopped being a box in front of the camera and
# became a volume the ship is inside, and most of what this block used to say
# about them went with it. What each case was *for* had been written down in
# terms of the star band's own machinery — the fast paths that reduced to
# identities when the camera was abeam, the sign of the depth travel, the ramp
# the near wall needed, the fold that carried a trail across the edge — and
# there is no band any more, so none of those reasons survives its subject.
#
# What the spread is for now is simpler and mostly unchanged: ten flights over
# one sky, at both speeds, from six camera angles, in three colour modes, with
# and without a hand on the stick. Read it as a grid rather than as a list of
# special cases.
#
#   Ramp.         Seven of the ten moved when a streak stopped ending at a
#                 third of its head's brightness and started running out at
#                 nothing. The three that did not are the 60 fps `--demo` runs,
#                 whose exposure is an exact zero: every streak is a point and
#                 the ramp is never evaluated at all.
#
#                 The floor was right while a streak was one step of motion —
#                 a tail was where the star had been a frame ago, and nothing
#                 ever left through it. A streak is an exposure seconds long
#                 now, so the tail is where the shutter closes and everything
#                 ageing out of the picture leaves through exactly that edge.
#                 A third of full brightness there is a bright edge dragged
#                 across the frame: hold a turn at low warp, where the whole
#                 trail is the smear the turn left, and the sky holds its
#                 streaks for a second after the stick comes back and is then
#                 wiped. One ramp, running out at nothing, is what makes a
#                 trail thin away as it ages.
#
#   Falloff.      Five of the ten moved when a streak stopped being measured by
#                 the window it is seen through and started being measured by
#                 the track it flew — `warp.txt`, `side.txt`, `astern.txt`,
#                 `steer.txt` and `ahead.txt`. What separates the five that
#                 moved from the five that did not is worth reading, because
#                 neither half is what it looks like. The three 60 fps `--demo`
#                 flights never leave sublight, so their exposure is an exact
#                 zero, every streak is a point and the falloff is never
#                 applied at all: those three are untouched by construction.
#                 `orbit.txt` and `drift.txt` are the interesting pair. The
#                 change reaches them — the same two flights at 300x90 come out
#                 wholly different — but at 120x36 it moves not one eight-bit
#                 level, because most of what the bubble reaches in an exterior
#                 frame already went through `draw_path`, which was measuring
#                 the whole streak all along. So they are not a control saying
#                 the change stayed put; they are two framings where it is too
#                 small to see. The property that says it happened is in
#                 `canvas.rs`, where two terminals of different widths are made
#                 to draw the same streak and compared.
#
#   Colour.       `truecolor.txt` and `ansi256.txt` are the same flight in two
#                 spellings, so a change that moves one and not the other has
#                 landed in the writer rather than in the renderer. Detection is
#                 gone and nothing is handed the palette mode by default, which
#                 makes `ansi256.txt` the only guard on `quantize_256` and the
#                 palette-index path. `ascii.txt` is the third, and the only one
#                 that says nothing outside printable ASCII was emitted.
#
#   Speed.        The four `--demo` runs and the four `--engage` ones split the
#                 sky's whole dynamic range, and that split is sharper than it
#                 used to be: at impulse the stars now hold *still* — a full
#                 impulse frame differs from a parked one only by the twinkle —
#                 while at warp the exposure draws three seconds of each star's
#                 own track. A change to the exposure moves the flights that
#                 light a drive and leaves the rest alone — seven of the ten
#                 now, and the count is measured rather than asserted: this
#                 block said five until something finally tested it. Pacing the
#                 exposure to the distance actually flown moved all four
#                 `--engage` runs and both `--fps 10` ones, whose drive lights
#                 at frame 60 of a twelve-second flight, while `truecolor.txt`,
#                 `ascii.txt` and `ansi256.txt` came out byte for byte
#                 identical: two seconds of `--demo` at 60 fps never leaves
#                 sublight, and at sublight the exposure is an exact zero.
#
#                 Lighting a drive is not the same as spooling one up, which is
#                 what `ahead.txt` is here to say. Every `--engage` flight but
#                 that one lasts two seconds, in which the ship covers only a
#                 few light years, so the exposure never reaches far enough for
#                 anything that depends on its *length* to happen at all. That
#                 is how a bug that lost the trail of every star inside the
#                 exposure — a fifth of the pool — moved not one of the nine
#                 hashes that existed before this case was added.
#
#   Camera.       Five flights watch from outside and they are at five
#                 different angles, which is now a question about which sky is
#                 in front of the lens rather than about which arithmetic is
#                 switched on. `side.txt` is abeam, where `Eye::to_camera` is an
#                 exact swizzle and the hull's axis lies flat in the image
#                 plane, so it is still the shot with no vanishing point at all
#                 and still the one the engine lance's clamp cannot move.
#
#                 What the angle decides for the *sky* is the sign of
#                 `Orbit::nose_in_camera`'s depth component,
#                 `-cos(elevation)·sin(azimuth)`: behind the ship the exposure
#                 recedes and ahead of it the exposure runs at the lens and has
#                 to be cut against the near plane. `astern.txt` holds one side
#                 of that and `ahead.txt` the other, at `--orbit 90,0,0` where
#                 the nose points straight down the barrel and the cut bites
#                 hardest. `orbit.txt` is nominally on the forward side too and
#                 cannot substitute: two seconds is not long enough an exposure
#                 for the cut to reach any star, which is the whole of why
#                 `ahead.txt` runs at `--fps 10`.
#
#   Stick.        The four `--demo` flights steer and the four `--engage` ones
#                 hold every rate at an exact zero from the first frame to the
#                 last. That distinction reaches further than it did twice over.
#                 The ship carries an attitude, and the sky is projected through
#                 it, so a turn moves the sky from *both* cameras rather than
#                 only from the seat — `drift.txt` is the flight that sees that
#                 from outside. And a turn now reaches the exposure's own
#                 geometry: a streak is the track a star swept while the shutter
#                 was open, so one the ship flew straight through is a segment
#                 and one it turned through is a curve.
#
#                 That second half is what makes the `--engage` four a control
#                 rather than merely a contrast. They hold `LEVEL_AXES` to the
#                 bit from the first frame to the last, so their exposure never
#                 reaches back past a turn and the sky takes the arithmetic it
#                 always took — which is why drawing the exposure along the
#                 flown track moved `steer.txt` and `drift.txt` and left the
#                 other eight byte for byte. Anything else moving with a change
#                 to the track has leaked; either of those two failing to move
#                 means the weave is not steering at warp and the reference is
#                 watching nothing.
#
#                 What the pair do *not* cover is the shape of a curve. The
#                 weave turns about a twentieth of what a hand on the stick
#                 does, which asks for a single leg — the right two endpoints
#                 with a straight line between them. The curve itself is pinned
#                 by property tests in `universe.rs`, which is the level it
#                 belongs at: it is a variant, not a region.
#
#   Autopilot.    `drift.txt` shares every flag with `steer.txt` but the view,
#                 which makes the pair one flight watched from inside and from
#                 outside — the relationship `ansi256.txt` has to
#                 `truecolor.txt`, applied to a camera instead of to a colour
#                 mode. It is parked aft of the beam rather than on it so the
#                 camera *crosses* the beam mid-flight, at frame 72 with the
#                 drive lit since frame 60, which is the band the drive swaps
#                 sides over traversed as a ramp rather than sat beside.
#
#                 That angle is derived from `autopilot::CAMERA_TURN` and has to
#                 be re-derived whenever that moves: it is how far the camera
#                 walks in the seconds before the crossing should land, and the
#                 crossing has to land after the drive lights at frame 60. It
#                 was -60,0,0 at a turn every 43 seconds and -20,0,0 at every
#                 137.
#
# None of the ten names a sky, so all of them fly the default limiting
# magnitude — which is a fixed number of stars over the whole celestial sphere
# on every terminal, where `--stars` was a fixed number *drawn*. The reference
# is also still one ship deep: no case passes `--ship`, so all five flights
# with a hull in them fly the enterprise, and a fault that only some other
# outline exposes wants a property test over `models()` rather than an eleventh
# flight.
#
# The hashes hold across build profiles and rustc versions but not across
# platforms: `sin`, `cos`, `exp` and `powf` come from the system maths library,
# and recycling the sky turns a one-ulp difference into a different universe.
# So they are checked on `ubuntu-latest` only, and `tests/golden.rs` is gated to
# Linux for the same reason.
#
# Regenerating: build a release binary and run the ten commands in `CLAUDE.md`,
# then put this block back. Say in the commit message which moved and why.
